\deflang1036\pard\qc\plain\f4\fs48\b ******  Polaris 3.1  ******
\par \pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24 
\par \pard\plain\f4\fs28\b Presenting Polaris \plain\f4\fs24\b    
\par \pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24 
\par Polaris is a shareware package for the Psion series 5/5mx. It was written for star gazers and amateur astronomes who want to know which celestial bodies are visible from a chosen spot, at a given moment in time.
\par 
\par Polaris establishes a map of the sky at a specified height from any viewpoint and supplies alt-azimutal markers.
\par Reminder :
\par Altitude or height is given in degrees ;
\par 0\'b0 = Horizon
\par 90\'b0 = zenith.
\par The azimuth is the horizontal direction.
\par       0\'b0 = North
\par    90\'b0 = East
\par 180\'b0 = South
\par 270\'b0 = West
\par 
\par Several zoom factors are available (0-15), allowing the user to select different sized portions of the sky. The database includes the principal stars and their proper names for constructing the constellations, the secondary stars, the 110 Messier objects and a planetary position calculation module.
\par 
\par A new module was added to the version 1.1 which allows the user to edit and manage some files of celestial bodies. Each map is vectorially calculated from the information contained in the database. This implies a lot of work for the CPU, and takes some time, but gives the user access to very powerful functions and a constant precision
\par independent of the chosen zoom factor.
\par 
\par The 3D projective view of celestial sphere allows a correct azimutal representation of sky and constellations patterns. To take the weight off the CPU during calculations, Planetary positions are given to the nearest 0.2\'b0 and sunrise sunset times are given to the nearest 3 minutes, which is OK for this application whose goal in life is to satisfy such questions as ; "What will I look at tonight or next week in a given portion of the sky ?"
\par 
\par  Polaris features functions normally available only on more powerful machines (such as a PC, etc.), including :
\par 
\par \bullet  Point and click interface to obtain celestial and alta-azimutal coordinates of any object or point on the screen.
\par \bullet  A viewpoint which can be moved towards an object, planet, etc.
\par \bullet  Choice in what should be shown on a given map.
\par 
\par To try out Polaris, load the sample view ' Planet conjonction March 2000 '  and go towards the MOON.
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain\f4\fs28\b Installing Polaris 
\par 
\par Distribution Files : 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\b POLARIS.SIS  \plain\f4\fs24 program installer french/english.
\par \pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24\b OPTION.SIS    \plain\f4\fs24 optional file, see further in the text.
\par \plain\f4\fs24\b Lisezmoi.rtf \tab \plain\f4\fs24 french manual
\par \plain\f4\fs24\b Readme.rtf \tab \plain\f4\fs24 this file.
\par \plain\f4\fs24\b 
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Just click twice on \plain\f4\fs24\b POLARIS.SIS\plain\f4\fs24  file from you PC if you have \plain\f4\fs24\b PsiWin\plain\f4\fs24  installed or copy the file on the Psion and run it. You'll be prompted to choose langage and other installation options.
\par \pard\li720\fi-360\plain\f4\fs24  
\par \pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24 The following files are  catalogues of celestial bodies which are of some interest for an amateur astronomer equipped with only simple binoculars or more powerful instruments. They are copied by default in the program directory
\par 
\par \pard\li507\fi-283\tx507\plain\f5\fs24 \bullet \plain\f6\fs24\b NGC_12.FIC\plain\f6\fs24   a selection of the New General Catalogue of objects such as galaxies, clusters and nebulae with magnitude brighter than 12.0. It contains 1854 objects but if you are interested by fainter objects the installation file \plain\f6\fs24\b OPTION.SIS \plain\f6\fs24 proposes you \plain\f6\fs24\b NGC_15.FIC\plain\f6\fs24  which contains 7474 objects up to magnitude 15.0.\plain\f0\fs24 
\par \plain\f5\fs24 \bullet \plain\f6\fs24  \plain\f0\fs24\b CONSTELL.FIC\plain\f0\fs24  ( by Eberhard Rompf) displays constellations names.
\par \plain\f5\fs24 \bullet \plain\f6\fs24  \plain\f0\fs24\b STARNAME.FIC\plain\f0\fs24  ( by Eberhard Rompf) displays some major stars names.
\par \plain\f5\fs24\b \bullet \plain\f6\fs24\b DOUBLE.FIC \plain\f0\fs24 a catalogue of more than 1500 double stars which responds to these criteria:\plain\f0\fs24\b 
\par \pard\li734\fi-283\tx734\plain\f5\fs24 \endash \plain\f6\fs24 the main star have his magnitude <= 6.0\plain\f0\fs24 
\par \plain\f5\fs24 \endash \plain\f6\fs24 the second star have his magnitude <=13.0\plain\f0\fs24 
\par \plain\f5\fs24 \endash \plain\f6\fs24 the distance is >= 1 Arcsec\plain\f0\fs24 
\par \plain\f5\fs24 \endash \plain\f6\fs24 the distance is <= 2 Arcmn\plain\f0\fs24 
\par \pard\li507\fi-283\tx507\plain\f5\fs24 \bullet \plain\f6\fs24 Some comments have been added to achieve easy filtering of interesting pairs (see \plain\f0\fs24\b Abbreviation\plain\f0\fs24  section in the help file)
\par \pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24 
\par The extension '.ORB' is used for comets & asteroids Orbital elements. Next files are to be copied depending of your interest and of the available space on your disks.
\par \pard\li507\fi-283\tx507\plain\f5\fs24 \bullet \plain\f6\fs24\b COMET.ORB\plain\f6\fs24  contains some recently observed comets including Hale Bopp is copied by default .\plain\f0\fs24 
\par \plain\f5\fs24 \bullet \plain\f6\fs24\b ASTER100.ORB\plain\f6\fs24   contains 238 asteroids of  dimension more than 100 Km will be copied by default.\plain\f0\fs24 
\par \pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24 
\par if you run the installation of \plain\f4\fs24\b Option files\plain\f4\fs24 
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\b ASTER50.ORB\plain\f4\fs24   contains 688 asteroids of  dimension more than 50 Km.
\par \plain\f4\fs24\b ASTER10.ORB\plain\f4\fs24   contains 1766 asteroids of  dimension more than 10 Km.
\par 
\par If you are interested in comets and asteroids you can browse on my web site :
\par http://members.aol.com/achay/index.htm.
\par 
\par There are useful links to often updated databases of orbital elements and \plain\lang2057\f4\fs24 particularly\plain\f4\fs24  to TED BOWELL's site where daily updated asteroids elements are available,  I also provide some tools to read these files and to translate them to Polaris ORB file.
\par 
\par \pard\plain\f4\fs28\b Using Polaris
\par \plain\f4\fs24\b   
\par \pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24\b Important\plain\f4\fs24 : most of the subjects explained here are included in the help file provided with the program
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\b General 
\par \plain\f4\fs24   
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul The Screen
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Polaris draws a 640x640 pixel chart, this speeds up the program by avoiding rezooming, and also provides a more natural square chart especially a nice whole sky view. The Psion screen displays a 640x240 pixel zone of this chart, so you have to scroll the display to see the entire chart.
\par \pard\li720\fi-360\plain\f4\fs24 'Up and Down' arrows scroll the screen.
\par 'PgUp' and 'PgDown' combinations goes to the upper and downer zones.
\par 'Enter' key goes to the central zone.
\par \pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Keyboard and Hotkeys
\par \plain\f4\fs24 The use of 'Ctrl+key' combination is not necessary but works, to access hotkeys type directly the desired key or 'Cap'+key combination.
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Scroll bar
\par \plain\f4\fs24 The scroll bar at the left indicates the current relative displayed zone. 
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Cursor
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Some commands require you to click at a point on the screen, a flashing cursor then appears. You can move it with the arrow keys. Press ' Enter ' to confirm or ' Esc ' to cancel the current command and hide the cursor. You can also just point on the screen with the Pen. After some commands it stays on the screen in a ' static ' form to let you remind the last point entered.
\par 'Esc' key also hides it after a 'Show with cursor' option in 'Search' command.
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\b Menu  Files 
\par \plain\f4\fs24 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Catalogue
\par \plain\f4\fs24  Description:  search, read and edit items of a collection of celestial bodies. In the main window the first line displays the name of the current item, the second one displays its screen name. In the small rectangle are displayed: the name of the current file, the rank of the current item  and the total number of items. The following commands are available:
\par \pard\plain\f6\fs24 "<-" Moves to the previous item.
\par "->" Moves to the next  item.
\par "Tab" Searches for a string in all files.
\par "+" Continuous search.
\par "E" Edits the current item.
\par "Esc" Returns to sky window and saves the current file.
\par "Del" Deletes current item, be careful, as no confirmation is asked.
\par "N" Edits a new item.
\par "Menu" Displays a short menu with some options: open  another file of object, save the current with a different  name.
\par \pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Comets and Asteroids
\par \plain\f4\fs24 This allows you to manage some files of Orbital elements used to compute comets or asteroids positions. You can add new objects like incoming comets or update asteroids orbital elements. (see Iras subject on my web site).
\par The edition of orbital parameters varies depending on what data are available. The four major cases are:
\par 
\par \pard\li283\fi-283\tx283\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24 1) The time at Perihelion is known:
\par \pard\li453\fi-170\tx453\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24 a) It is given in julian date format and can be typed directly.
\par b) the format is Day Month Year and Time: leave this value to 0 and fill the other fields. When saving an additional dialogue will allows you to enter the date. The 'Mean Anomaly' must be ignored.
\par 
\par \pard\li283\fi-283\tx283\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24 2) The time at Perihelion is unknown:
\par \pard\li453\fi-170\tx453\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24 Leave this value to 0. When saving additional dialogue lets you enter a 'Mean Anomaly' value and the Date at which it is computed. Note that in this case the Semi Major Axis must be known.
\par 
\par \pard\li283\fi-283\tx283\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24 3) The distance at Perihelion is given:
\par \pard\li453\fi-170\tx453\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24 Fill this field and leave 'Semi major Axis' to 0.
\par 
\par \pard\li283\fi-283\tx283\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24 4) The semi major axis is known:
\par \pard\li453\fi-170\tx453\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24 Fill this field and leave 'Distance at Perihelion'' to 0.
\par 
\par \pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24\i\ul View
\par \plain\f4\fs24 You can load a previously saved view or save the current one. When loading one you are asked if the saved time must be used (i.e.  an eclipse view needs to recover the saved time) or if the current time must be used (i.e. South view at zoom 3 at home) .
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Printing
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Prints the current view. The poor definition of the screen comparing to printer's give a so poor result but I study a way too have a better print output.
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Registration
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Allows input of user's code and name for access to the non-limited version.
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul About
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Presentation screen with version and registration status.
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Quit
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Leaves Polaris.
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\b Menu Settings
\par \plain\f4\fs24 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Starting
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Allows you to set the starting parameter like: Location, Catalogue file, zoom and direction, 'Show' options etc...
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Date &Time
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Allows input of the observation time, a 'Now' option sets it to current time and date. The "Autom" option forces display update calculations every 6 minutes (available only when time is set to "Now").
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Location
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Allows 'Load', 'Edit' and 'Create' commands for Location .
\par Longitude and latitude values are expressed in decimal degrees.
\par 
\par \bullet  Longitude East is expressed using positive values.
\par \bullet  Longitude West is expressed using negative values.
\par \bullet  Latitude North is expressed using positive values.
\par \bullet  Latitude South is expressed using negative values.
\par \bullet  DTU indicates the difference (expressed in hours) between local time and GMT.
\par Daylight saving time must be set to 'Yes' if you want Polaris to take account of it, you may save the same location with two different names and one with this option active and not active for the other like 'Paris winter' and 'Paris summer'.
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Show
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Allows choice of which objects to show/hide on the display. Some have only 'Yes,No' choice but some have a third choice:
\par 
\par \pard\li720\fi-360\plain\f4\fs24 'Constellations' + 'Stars' : draws Constellation stars but not their patterns
\par 'Grids' + 'Horizon': draws only the horizon line (in black).
\par 'Catalogue' +'Filter': This option allows you to define a filtering of catalogue objects by magnitude and string search. If selected an additional dialogue lets you edit these values after ending the 'Show' command. How works the filter ? It displays only objects that match an upper limit magnitude and that contains a string. (i.e. To display only Open Cluster brighter than 10.5 Mv type 10.5 and 'open' and choose 'Clusteng.fic' as catalogue file)
\par 
\par \pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Centre
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Requires user to click with the cursor at a point on the screen and centres the view on this point.
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Zoom
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Requires user to click with the cursor at a point on the screen and then to select a zoom factor (1 to 15).
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Status
\par \plain\f4\fs24 This gives you the current status: current time (the time used for computation, not necessarily the real one ),
\par the current location, the catalogue and comet/asteroid files.
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\b Orientation
\par \plain\f4\fs24 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul 'North, East, South,West
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Standard views along these directions at 25\'b0 above the horizon (the black line) and with a 150\'b0 field.
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Zenith
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Standard zenital view with 180\'b0 field.
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Direction
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Observation direction and Zoom factor can be specified here in azimuth and height.
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Search
\par \plain\f4\fs24 This command allows you to find various types of objects and centres the display on them. In some situations the requested object already lies on the screen, thus, to avoid a display refreshing and spare time, a 'Show with cursor' option is supplied. 
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i Planet
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Centres the map on a planet, i.e. 7 planets + sun + moon
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i Constellation
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Centres the map on a constellation: an interface lets you 'navigate' through the constellations or search a name in the database: ex. input 'Bear' will return "Great bear" and "Little Bear " ...). See 'Catalogue' for commands.
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i Messier
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Centres the map on an object from the Messier Catalogue: M1 to M109.
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i Catalogue
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Centres the map on an Object from the catalogue file like the Ngc_12.fic.
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i Star
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Centres the map on a star, if you know the name of a star you can find it on the map or just go through the database by the arrow keys. If you press ' Ctrl' while pressing the arrow keys you may jump to the next or previous constellation. See 'Catalogue' for commands.
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\b Menu Info
\par \plain\f4\fs24 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Ephemeris
\par \plain\f4\fs24 The ephemeris of Planets, Sun and Moon or Asteroids and Comets are given for the current date. For asteroids and comets the ephemeris concerns the current object, additional dialogues allows you to change the current one.
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Constellation
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Requires user to click with the pointer at a point on the screen and returns the name of the nearest star giving its name, the constellation to which it belongs, its visual magnitude and equatorial coordinates for the current epoch.
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Coordinates
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Requires user to click with the pointer at a point on the screen and returns the equatorial coordinates of the point, and also the culmination, rising and setting times in Local Time.
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Azimuth
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Requires user to click with the pointer at a point on the screen and returns the azimuthal coordinates of the point.
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Jovian Moons
\par \plain\f4\fs24 This displays three windows. In the right one and the upper one \plain\f4\fs24\cf0 Jupiter \plain\f4\fs24 is drawn and its four major satellites. In the third one the available commands \plain\f4\fs24\cf0 are grouped\plain\f4\fs24 .
\par 
\par ' Polar view ' displays a view of Jupiter's moons \plain\lang2057\f4\fs24 perpendicularly\plain\f4\fs24  of their orbital plane. This is to appreciate the orbital distances that are scaled with Jupiter diameter. The arrow indicates the direction of the Earth.
\par 
\par ' Terrestrial view ' offers a realistic view of these objects seen from the Earth.
\par 
\par The command window offers:
\par \pard\li720\fi-360\plain\f4\fs24 'I': this toggles between direct or inverted terrestrial view. ( most astronomy optics invert the vision, but binoculars made for terrestrial use don't).
\par 'D':  allows to change the date of computation.
\par 'Up' and 'Down': change the time increment value used by animation features ( 30mn, 1h, 6h, 1 day, 4 days).
\par 'Right' and 'Left': applies time increment or decrement.
\par 'F' and 'B': starts a forward or back animation.
\par 'Esc': return to Polaris chart.
\par 
\par \pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Differential
\par \plain\f4\fs24 This feature is useful for those who have a scope equipped with Right Ascension and Declination circle but no pointing computer. It asks you to point out a reference point ( a bright easy to find star) and to point at a desired object, the difference of the celestial coordinates are then given.
\par Note: a positive difference of Declination means ' Norther' and positive difference of Right Ascension means in the diurnal way.
\par 
\par \plain\f4\fs24\i\ul Symbols
\par \plain\f4\fs24 Shows the symbols used for displaying stars and planets.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain\f4\fs24\b Specifications
\par \pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24 
\par \pard\li720\fi-360\plain\f4\fs24 \bullet  The valid range for computation dates is from the 1st of January 1901 to the31st of December 2079 (Registered Version).
\par \bullet  Accuracy of Planets position 0.2\'b0 for R.A. and 0.06\'b0 for Declination.
\par \bullet  Accuracy of Moon position 0.2\'b0 for RA and 0.06\'b0 for Decl.
\par \bullet  Accuracy of Sun position 0.01\'b0 for both RA and Decl.
\par \bullet  780 Stars in the constellations database.
\par \bullet  16000 secondary stars for magnitude  equal or brighter than  7.0
\par \bullet  Time required to display objects : 1s for 160 Obj. approx.
\par \bullet  The formats accepted by the database for coordinates are:
\par \pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24        Right Ascension: 23.4567h or 23h 27.67 m or 23h 27 m24s (Unused spaces are deleted)
\par        Declination: 23.25\'b0 ou 23\'b015' or 23\'b015' 15" (Unused spaces are deleted)
\par \pard\li720\fi-360\plain\f4\fs24 
\par \pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\plain\f4\fs24 
